// Deal or Force - the staff that keeps a front a front.
//
// Once a day, and at once for a front that has just moved, every country's sector of every
// front is looked at as a whole: every post held before any post is held twice, the posts
// facing the enemy first, and a pile given up to the holes nearest it. A post's share of the
// divisions is its share of the pressure on the line - one for being on the front, and one
// more for every division's worth of the enemy standing next to it.
//
// The same staff keeps the posts of a player's divisions ordered to defend a front, and the
// lines a player draws. Spearheads, divisions in a battle and divisions on a direct order
// are never moved.
//
// Every file in this folder is read in name order and later files win.
{
  // How many divisions it may re-post at one look: this many, plus this share of the posts.
  "FrontRepostsPerLook": 3,
  "FrontRepostsPerPost": 0.15,

  // Days a division just posted is left alone, unless the post it is on is well over its share.
  "FrontSettleDays": 3,

  // With fewer divisions than posts, a lone division leaves a quiet post for an empty one
  // only when the empty one faces this many times the pressure.
  "FrontThinQuietRatio": 3,

  // Whether divisions ordered to advance are spread along the line as well as those ordered
  // to defend. Off: an advance stays where the planner massed it.
  "FrontStaffMovesAdvances": false,

  // Whether a division of a country at war that serves none of its wars - raised during it, or
  // released when another war ended - is told to hold the line of the nearest front at once,
  // rather than standing where it is until that front next moves.
  "FrontStaffCallsUpIdle": true
}
